WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.678
[MUSIC]

00:00:10.678 --> 00:00:14.654
If you look at ethics, for one, most
people's sense of value and

00:00:14.654 --> 00:00:19.646
what's important to them, and how to act
comes from their religious tradition,

00:00:19.646 --> 00:00:23.913
or from some philosophical perspective
that they may have gained

00:00:23.913 --> 00:00:28.835
through humanistically not necessarily
religiously, but from their family or

00:00:28.835 --> 00:00:30.785
their community.

00:00:30.785 --> 00:00:32.735
So, it's, it's natural,

00:00:32.735 --> 00:00:37.640
it seems to me, to have that as part of
what you're doing in your work life.

00:00:37.640 --> 00:00:42.670
Because that's how you ultimately operate
when the chips are down,

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:47.640
when you're being asked to be honest, when
you see things going on that are wrong.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.760
Your religion really is there behind you I
think, or your spirituality.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:56.970
But in other ways, too, just to, to find
times in the day when you can relax,

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:01.760
when you can pull back from all the stress
of work.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.502
Spirituality, especially through
meditation prayer,

00:01:05.502 --> 00:01:09.163
other modalities like that, is a way for
you to, again,

00:01:09.163 --> 00:01:14.451
find what's really important, find your
essence in the midst of a very busy day.

00:01:14.451 --> 00:01:19.163
[MUSIC]

00:01:19.163 --> 00:01:23.864
If you're constantly busy, if you're
constantly going, going, going,

00:01:23.864 --> 00:01:27.670
you really do, first of all, start making
mistakes.

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.690
You don't have perspective.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:35.670
You don't have the vision that you often
need strategically to think about your

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:41.320
work, and about the firm as a whole and
where it fits in, in industry and society.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.520
And you get tired out.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:44.780
I think it's a back and forth.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:47.520
It's, as the Taoist tradition would say, a
yin and yang.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.140
You want to go back from moments of quiet
and

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:55.930
stillness to times of being very busy and
very productive.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:02:00.490
And you would do better in terms of
integrating yourself as a whole being.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:02.790
But also you'd be doing better in your
workplace.

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:07.670
[MUSIC]

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:11.961
My parents used to tell me, there's two
things you should never talk about in

00:02:11.961 --> 00:02:17.270
public, especially over dinner with
friends, and that's religion and politics.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.130
So I think that's right.

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:21.520
People are worried about where religion
may lead.

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:22.430
It's very deep.

00:02:22.430 --> 00:02:23.660
It's very powerful.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.270
It can be very divisive.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:29.060
There's terrible things that are done in
the name of religion and currently being

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:33.030
done in the name of religion, so there's
lots of reasons to be leery of it.

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.920
In the book that I wrote with Lauren Ash,

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:40.180
at Harvard Business School, we look at
three kinds of religion.

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:45.660
What we call espoused religion, catalytic
religion, and foundational religion.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:49.300
And by espoused religion we do mean the
dogmas and the doctrines, and

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.740
the teachings of a particular tradition.

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:54.996
And often those traditions, like with
Christianity, or with Islam,

00:02:54.996 --> 00:02:59.520
are proselytizing and converting
traditions, and

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.670
we think it's not appropriate to bring
that to the workplace.

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:07.470
But catalytic religion, by which we mean
these methodologies, meditation and

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:09.920
prayer are things that can be personally
sustaining, and

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:11.770
which you can do, honestly, in private.

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:14.720
You know, you can close your own door or
go out, outside by yourself.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.520
You don't have to be seen doing that if
that doesn't feel cool.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:20.640
And then there's foundational religion.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:25.730
And by that we mean the great stories,
sagas rules that we all

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:30.162
kind of agree with, like the Golden Rule
or the Ten Commandments.

00:03:30.162 --> 00:03:33.664
Those you can usually bring into the
workplace and

00:03:33.664 --> 00:03:37.912
even note that they come from sources like
the Hebrew Bible or

00:03:37.912 --> 00:03:42.014
something, and not have that be
problematic for people.

00:03:42.014 --> 00:03:44.391
[MUSIC]

00:03:44.391 --> 00:03:47.966
If you're gonna do business globally, it
really does behoove you to know what

00:03:47.966 --> 00:03:51.811
the religious tradition is, that's primary
within that tradition or, or several

00:03:51.811 --> 00:03:55.570
traditions, several religious traditions
maybe that are within that country.

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:58.760
So if you're doing business in Egypt, it
does behoove you to know something about

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:02.970
Islam, if you're doing business in India,
something about Hinduism, and so on.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:07.760
Because so much of the business climate,
in terms of its etiquette, it's the ethos,

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:10.520
the cultural ethos of, of a business,

00:04:10.520 --> 00:04:14.430
does depend on the underlying religion
which is often hidden from view.

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:17.675
But if you know something about it it'll,
it'll help you a lot.

00:04:17.675 --> 00:04:22.346
[MUSIC]

